## ABSTRACT
Photo-induced vacancy defects are employed strategically to imbue semiconductors with enhanced performance characteristics for many important applications such as surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) sensing, photocatalysis, and photovoltaic applications. However, the long-term maintenance and use of photo-induced vacancy defects remain elusive, because of their rapid self-healing upon air exposure. In this study, we demonstrate that photo-induced oxygen vacancy $(\mathsf{P I V}_{\mathsf{O}})$ defects can be stabilized  by  the  photoexcitation  of  metal–organic  framework  (MOF)  materials,  which  is  crucial  for  SERS  analysis.  The $\mathsf{P I V}_{\mathrm{O}}$ defects  in  MOF  materials  are  stable  for  at  least  two  weeks  in  the  ambient  atmosphere,  owing  to  the  combination  of  steric hindrance and electron delocalization around vacancy defects, which significantly contrasts the short lifetime (within minutes) of $\mathsf{P I V}_{\mathrm{O}}$ defects  in  metal-oxide  semiconductors.  With  the  formation  of  stable $\mathsf{P I V}_{\mathrm{O}}$ defects,  a  prominent  SERS  enhancement surpassing  that  of  pristine  MOFs  is  achieved,  accompanied  with  a  reduced  limit  of  detection  by  three  orders  of  magnitude. Moreover, the additional SERS enhancement rendered by $\mathsf{P I V}_{\mathrm{O}}$ defects  can  be  stably  retained  and  is  effective  for  monitoring various small molecules, such as dopamine and bisphenol A.
## KEYWORDS
stable  photo-induced  vacancy  defects,  metal−organic  framework  materials,  steric  hindrance,  electron  delocalization, Raman spectroscopy
## Introduction
Vacancy  defect  engineering  in  semiconductors  is  an  interesting and important topic from the viewpoint of semiconductor physics and applications such as photocatalysis, photovoltaic, and sensing applications [1–6]. Both theoretical and experimental studies have revealed  that  vacancy  defects  affect  the  optical  absorption, separation,  and  migration  of  photogenerated  carriers,  as  well  as the electrical conductivity and surface properties of semiconductors; hence, they significantly affect the applications of semiconductors  [1, 7, 8]. As  an  example,  vacancy  defects  have revolutionized for semiconductor-based surface-enhanced Raman scattering (SERS) substrates in recent years. It was discovered that the  SERS  sensitivity  of  semiconductors  can  be  increased significantly  up  to $\sim10^{5}–10^{7}$ by  the  introduction  of  a  suitable density  of  oxygen  vacancy  defects;  this  strongly  suggests  that vacancy  defects  are  critical  in  improving  the  SERS  activity  of semiconductors  [9–11].  Fundamentally,  two  types  of  vacancy defects are generated from different origins, i.e., native defects and defects  initiated  by  external  excitation  [12].  Native  defects  are intrinsically  introduced  during  the  synthesis  of  semiconductors, whereas  defects  initiated  by  external  excitation  can  be  created because of other external-field-induced effects such as high-energy light irradiation—these defects are termed photo-induced defects.
Photo-induced  defects,  which  are  associated  with  exciton–lattice interactions, are an important origin of nonequilibrium states that dynamically  exist  in  defective  materials,  which  typically  exhibit unique  electronic  properties  that  differ  significantly  from  native defects with fixed electronic states [12]. More importantly, photoinduced  vacancy  defects  are  advantageous  in  terms  of  their universal applicability to multiple semiconductor materials, which affects the SERS behavior of semiconductors, particularly in view of  charge-transfer  induced  enhancement.  However,  previous advances  in  semiconductor-based  SERS  rely  overwhelmingly upon  the  introduction  of  native  defects,  whereas  research pertaining  to  photo-induced  vacancy  defects  is  scarce;  this  is primarily due to the instability of photo-induced vacancy defects in  semiconductors  upon  air  exposure  [2, 13, 14],  which  is definitely detrimental to SERS analysis. Therefore, the identification  of  effective  strategies  for  addressing  stable  photoinduced  vacancy  defects  to  improve  SERS  performance  is meaningful and of great scientific value.
Metal–organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  are  an  attractive  class  of porous crystalline materials comprising metal centers coordinated to  organic  ligands  for  forming  well-organized  frameworks  [15]. The  high  surface  area,  high  porosity,  structural  tunability,  and adequate chemical and thermal stabilities of MOFs render them promising  candidates  for  a  wide  range  of  potential  applications such  as  gas  storage  and  separation,  liquid  separation  and purification,  electrochemical  energy  storage,  catalysis,  and sensing  [16–18].  What  must  be  specially  stressed  here  is  that MOFs can serve as an emerging material platform for stabilizing photo-induced  vacancy  defects.  MOFs  can  undergo  effective electron–hole  separation  upon  photoexcitation,  which  enables them  to  behave  like  semiconductors,  where  the  photo-induced electrons are trapped and preserved in the metal nodes, resulting in  the  formation  of  photo-induced  mixed-valence  compounds; meanwhile,  oxygen  vacancy  defects  may  be  resulted  from  the breakage of the bonds between reductive metal sites and oxygen atoms.  However,  in  contrast  to  semiconductors,  the  unique structural  features  of  MOFs  stabilize  the  presence  of  photoinduced  vacancy  defects  in  MOFs:  (1)  The  ligand  specificity  of MOFs  can  cause  a  delocalization  of  the  electrons  around  the vacancy defects, thereby decreasing their electronegativity; (2) The dangling  hydroxyl  groups  in  the  ligands  generated  by  breaking metal–O  bonds  can  provide  protection  to  the  adjacent  defect center,  based  on  steric  hindrance  against  the  accessary  of atmospheric  oxygen  (Scheme  1).  In  such  a  configuration,  longlifetime photo-induced vacancy defects in MOFs can be achieved. The  presence  of  photo-induced  defects  in  MOFs  alters  the electronic energy states that facilitate charge transfer transitions for the substrate–analyte system, which contributes to the significantly improved  SERS  activity.  In  fact,  MOFs  have  ever  been  used  in SERS by employing their porous three-dimensional (3D) network structure  as  a  scaffold  for  metal  particles,  with  their  SERS performance  mainly  from  the  metal  particles.  However,  the inherent properties of MOFs as advanced SERS substrates are yet to be disclosed [19, 20].
In  this  study,  we  demonstrate  that  the  stabilization  of  photoinduced  oxygen  vacancy $\mathrm{(PIV_{O})}$ defects  can  be  achieved  by photoexcitation  of  MOF  materials,  which  is  crucial  for  SERS analysis. To achieve this aim, $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}.}\end{array}$ , a prototype Ti-based MOF material, was synthesized to demonstrate the extraordinary stability of $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects, which remain stable for more than two weeks owing to the combination of steric hindrance and electron delocalization around vacancy defects. By contrast, $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects are unstable in typical semiconductors (e.g., $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ), i.e., they vanish upon air exposure within minutes. As a result of stabilized $\mathrm{PIV_{o}}$ defects in defective MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ , prominent SERS enhancement surpassing  that  of  the  pristine $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can  be  achieved, accompanied  by  a  further  reduction  in  the  limit  of  detection (LOD) by three orders of magnitude for the detection of 4-tertbutyl catechol (4-TBC) as a probe molecule. The enhanced SERS activities in defective $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ are available up to 120 days after the generation of $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects for the detection of various small molecules, such as dopamine and bisphenol A. In fact, the photo-activated  strategy  is  widely  applicable  to  various  types  of MOF materials. Our findings indicate that $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects in MOFs result  in  modified  energy  bands  that  facilitate  both  the  intervalence  charge  transfer  within  MOFs  and  interfacial  chargetransfer  transitions,  which  are  crucial  to  SERS  in  terms  of  the chemical enhancement mechanism in the MOF–analyte system.
## 2    Results and discussion
## 2.1    Synthesis and characterization
## 2.2    Stable  SERS  behaviors  enabled  by  photo-induced oxygen vacancy defects
Dopamine,  which  functions  as  a  neurotransmitter  enable  pulse transmission in cells, was used as a prototype probe molecule to evaluate the strongly enhanced SERS activity of MOFs enabled by $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects.  In  fact,  the  rapid  and  sensitive  determination  of dopamine  is  vital  to  the  diagnosis  of  various  diseases  such  as Parkinson's disease [33, 34]. First, comparative experiments were conducted  by  recording  the  SERS  spectra  of  dopamine $(6.6\times10^{-4}\mathrm{M})$ adsorbed on bare $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Si},$ initial $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2},}\end{array}$ D$\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\textrm{(1h)}$ , D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ , photo-excited $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ particles,  and  colloidal $\mathrm{Ag}$ and  Au  nanoparticles  under  laser irradiation  at $532~\mathrm{nm}$ ,  as  shown  in Fig. 2(a).  Whereas  no  clear signals associated with dopamine molecules were observed on the bare $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}/\mathrm{Si}$ and Au nanoparticles, and an extremely weak signal was  observed  in  the  initial $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2},$ a  substantial  Raman enhancement was observed in D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $\mathrm{1h})$ , D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ ,  the $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles,  and  the  photo-excited $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ particles. Furthermore, the amplified SERS signals as a function of the illumination time can be clearly discerned in defective MIL${125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ . The prominent SERS signal at $1{,}486\ c m^{-1}$ obtained from D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ was  approximately  11  times  that  of  the pristine $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ substrate, indicating that the significance of $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects toward the SERS performance. Notably, D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (2 h) yielded a much stronger SERS response than the photoexcited $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ particle (more than five times greater), comparable with  that  of $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles,  whereas  the  extension  of  light irradiation time beyond $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ significantly diminished or completely terminated the SERS signal probably resulted from the collapse of the cluster-ligand framework topologies (Fig. S6 in the ESM). In particular, the SERS spectra of dopamine on D-MIL- $125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}\$ (2 h) were  dominated  by  three  intense  peaks  at  approximately  645, 1,334,  and $1{,}486~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ,  which  can  be  attributed  to  the $\mathrm{C-H}$ wagging,  asymmetric $\mathrm{C-H}$ twisting,  and  phenolic  vibrations, respectively [35, 36]. Notably, the $1{,}486\ c m^{-1}$ peak appeared as a new  peak  when  compared  with  the  normal  Raman  spectra  of dopamine powder, which is associated with the chemical bonding of  dopamine  to  surface  Ti  sites  via  phenolic  groups  [36] (Fig. 2(b)).  It  is  noteworthy  that  the  peak  positions  for  645  and $1{,}334\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ shifted considerably compared with those obtained for the pristine dopamine powder. The appearance of new peaks and the  shift  of  the  intrinsic  peaks  support  the  hypothesis  that  the SERS signal enhancement of D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(2\mathrm{h})$ is caused by the  molecule–surface  chemical  interaction  [37, 38].  The  SERS signals  of  dopamine  on ${\mathrm{D}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ were  further recorded against a concentration gradient ranging from $6.6\times10^{-4}$ to $6.6\times10^{-7}\mathrm{M}$ (Fig. 2(c)), with the lowest detectable concentration at $6.6~\times~10^{-7}$ M.  For  a  comparison,  the  lowest  detectable concentration of dopamine on the photo-excited $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ particle was $6.6\times10^{-5}\mathrm{~M~}$ (Fig. S7  in  the  ESM),  which  was  two  orders  of magnitude higher than that of D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{\cdotNH}_{2}$ (2 h).
In  addition  to  dopamine,  a  few  other  important  probe molecules  such  as  bisphenol  A  (BPA,  plasticizer),  4-TBC  (a polymerization inhibitor), and 5-hydroxymethylfurfural (5-HMF, biotoxic substance) were tested on the $\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ substrates, and similar results were obtained. For BPA and 5-HMF, a 10-fold increase  in  the  SERS  intensity  of  the $1,176\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ and  a  6-fold increase in the SERS intensity of the $1{,}618\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ were obtained on D-MIL- ${125-N\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ $(2\ \mathrm{~h~})$ at  a  concentration  of $8~\times~10^{-4}$ M, respectively, as compared with pristine MIL- ${125-N\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ (Figs. 3(a) and $3(\mathrm{b}))$ .  For  4-TBC,  the  minimum  LOD  of  D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ was $6.6\times10^{-7}\mathrm{~M},$ whereas  almost  no  SERS  signal  was detectable  on  pristine $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ even  at  a  relatively  large concentration of $6.6\times10^{-4}\mathrm{~M~}$ (Fig. 3(c) and Fig. S8 in the ESM), indicating a discrepancy of three orders of magnitude in sensitivity between  the  two  substrates.  In  addition  to $\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ similar enhanced SERS performances were also observed in other photoactivated  MOF  materials  such  as $\mathrm{{D}{-}M I L{-}}88{-}\mathrm{{N}H_{2}}$ and  DUiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (Figs. 3(d)–3(f), and Figs. S9 and S10 in the ESM, vibration  peak  mode  provided  in  the  ESM).  These  results collectively suggest that the generation of oxygen vacancy defects by  light  irradiation  is  an  effective  strategy  to  improve  the  SERS signal of MOF materials.
More  importantly,  the $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects  in  the  MOFs  exhibited excellent  long-term  stability,  rendering  them  suitable  for  SERS applications,  which  is  in  significant  contrast  to  the  rapid elimination of $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects in semiconductors, including those in the  presence  of  metallic  nanoparticles.  First,  the  changes  in  the SERS  intensity  of  photo-excited $\mathrm{\boldmath~\sf{MIL-125-NH}}_{2},$ ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ ,  and  Au$\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ stored in the ambient atmosphere (temperature: $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) were investigated  using  Raman  spectroscopy.  In  comparison  to  the intrinsic ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ no  further  enhancement  in  SERS  signals  of  BPA can  be  observed  on  the  photo-excited $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ substrate  upon  air exposure for $10~\mathrm{min}$ (Fig. S12 in the ESM), which is due to the rapid  elimination  of  photo-induced  oxygen  vacancy  defects  in ambient  atmosphere.  Upon  surface  modification  with  Au nanoparticles, the SERS signal from the photo-induced ${\mathrm{Au-TiO}}_{2}$ remained  for  one  hour  longer  compared  with  that  of  the unmodified sample (Figs. 4(a) and $4(\mathrm{b})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ); however, the additional time afforded was considered short.
Our  results  show  that  the  intensity  of  the  characteristic  peak $(1,176~\mathrm{cm^{-1}})$ of  BPA  on ${\mathrm{D}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{-}125{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ remained  stable over a period of 60 days at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ with only approximately $20\%$ attenuation  compared  with  the  initial  intensity.  Even  after  120 days, the SERS enhancement on $\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ${(2\mathrm{h})}$ remained superior  to  that  on $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2},$ indicating  that  the  partial maintenance of $\mathrm{PIV_{o}}$ defects had contributed to SERS activity in the  defective  MOFs  (Figs.  4(c) and $4(\mathrm{d}))$ .  In  addition  to  BPA, dopamine as a prototype probe molecule was tested on D-MIL${125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ (2 h) substrates, where similar reproducibility in terms of SERS activity obtained was achieved during substrate storage (Fig. S13 in the ESM). Furthermore, the $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects in D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ ,  after  heating  in  the  range  of $50\mathrm{-}100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $15~\mathrm{min}$ under  ambient  conditions,  remained  highly  active  during  the SERS detection of BPA. In particular, as illustrated by the intensity of the characteristic peak $(1,176\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ) of BPA on D-MIL- ${125-N\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ correlated  with  the  temperature  for  the  treatment  of  the substrate,  the  SERS  activity  of ${\mathrm{D}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ remained stable  at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ where  the  initial  intensity  barely  weakened. Above ${125}^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ the  signal  weakened  significantly,  with approximately $90\%$ attenuation.  For  comparison,  no  apparent SERS enhancement was observed on the photo-excited $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -Au compared with the initial SERS performance of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -Au after heating to $50{}^{\circ}\mathrm{C};$ this indicates that the SERS activity from photoinduced SERS enhancement is not available on the photo-excited $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -Au under this condition (Fig. S14 in the ESM). The results above all indicate that SERS behavior enabled by $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects in the MOFs offers a significant advantage over other semiconductors in terms of stability.
## 3    Mechanism
The  significantly  improved  stability  of  the $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects  in  the MOFs  can  be  explained  based  on  the  cluster-ligand  framework topologies of the MOFs, which assumes the coexistence of steric hindrance and electron delocalization around the oxygen vacancy defects. As illustrated in Fig. 5(a), the formation of photo-induced oxygen vacancies is initiated by charge carrier separation, where the holes are consumed by TEOA, leaving electrons at the Ti sites for the generation of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ in the Ti-oxo clusters. Oxygen vacancies may be generated around the reductive $\mathrm{Ti^{3+}}$ sites by the breakage of  Ti–O  bonds,  as  already  discovered  experimentally  and theoretically in varied MOFs [21]. As suggested by the results of density  functional  theory  (DFT)  calculations,  the  breakage  of Ti–O  bonds  between  the  reductive $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ sites  and  the  2- aminoterephthalic  ligand  may  be  favored  in  the  photo-excited $\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ based  on  its  relatively  low  system  energy $(E~=~-1{,}881{.}69~\mathrm{~eV}$ )  when  compared  with  other  possible configurations  (Fig. S15  in  the  ESM).  However,  coordination between $-\mathrm{O}-$ dangling  groups  in  the  ligand  and  the  adjacent metal center $(\mathrm{Ti}^{3+})$ may exist [21], which can exert electronegative surroundings  that  prevent  the  access  of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ upon  air  exposure. Such steric hindrance, typically absent in semiconducting oxides (e.g., $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ), contributes to the stabilization of $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects in the MOFs.  On  the  other  band,  electron  delocalization  around  the oxygen vacancy defects in the MOFs also has a vital impact on the stability of vacancy defects, which can be revealed by the calculated amount of charges carried by the Ti atoms adjacent to the $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects  (Fig. 5(b) and Fig. S16  in  the  ESM).  When  oxygen vacancies are introduced into ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2},$ the charges at neighboring Ti atoms are reduced significantly (from $2.25\mathrm{~e~}$ to 1.01 e for Ti1 site, from $2.25\mathrm{e}$ to 1.77 e for Ti2 site), suggesting the markedly reduced stability of the defective state, which is responsible for the rapid healing  of  oxygen  vacancies  in $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ upon  air  exposure.  By contrast, charges were altered slightly at the $\mathrm{Ti}$ atom sites (from $2.07\mathrm{~e~}$ to 1.97 e for Ti1 site, from $2.14\mathrm{~e~}$ to $2.11\mathrm{~e~}$ for Ti2 site, and from $1.95\mathrm{~e~}$ to  1.87  e  for  Ti3  site,  respectively)  when  a  similar oxygen-vacancy was introduction for the modeling structure of D$\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ; this clearly demonstrates the charge delocalization in  the  defective  structure,  which  contributed  to  the  improved stability of $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects in the MOFs.
The $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects  are  vital  to  the  enhancement  in  the  SERS effect based on charge-transfer coupling in the substrate–analyte system, as illustrated by the altered charge-transfer pathways when dopamine was combined with MIL- $125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . First, the strong interaction between dopamine and D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can  be  directly  observed  via  UV–Vis  spectroscopy,  as indicated by an emerging absorption band at $500{-}600~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. S17 in the ESM). The new absorption band, which is not observed in  the  MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ –dopamine  system,  indicates  the  formation of charge-transfer complexes enabled by defects in the D-MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ –dopamine system; this is a prerequisite for interfacial chargetransfer  transitions  that  is  crucial  for  achieving  enhanced  SERS activity.  Subsequently,  by  combining  the  results  from  UV–Vis absorption,  electrochemical  Mott–Schottky  analysis,  and  XPS valence  band  spectra  (Figs.  S18  and  S19),  the  valence  band maximum  (VBM)  and  conduction  band  minimum  (CBM)  for $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ and ${\mathrm{D}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ can  be  obtained,  as demonstrated by the locations with respect to the molecule levels (lowest  unoccupied  molecular  orbital  (LUMO)  and  highest occupied  molecular  orbital  (HOMO))  of  dopamine  (Fig. 5(c)). Owing  to  the  presence  of  photo-induced  defects,  an  upshifted VBM in addition to a slightly narrowed energy band $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ $(2.62\ \mathrm{eV})$ can  be  obtained  in $\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ as  compared  with pristine $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-NH}}_{2}$ $(E_{\mathrm{g}}=2.70~\mathrm{eV})$ .  The  altered  energy-band structure  of ${\mathrm{D}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ by  photo-induced  defects resulted in an improved band-level matching between the MOF substrate  and  the  dopamine  analyte,  as  indicated  by  the approximate  locations  of  the  CBM $(-6.28\mathrm{~\eV})$ and  HOMO $(-5.95~\mathrm{eV})$ ,  thereby  facilitating  the  ground-state  charge  transfer transitions $(\mu_{\mathrm{GSCT}})$ between  D-MIL- ${125-\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ $(2\ \mathrm{~h~})$ and  the chemisorbed dopamine molecules. Furthermore, a photo-induced charge transfer transition $(\mu_{\mathrm{PICT}})$ occurring at an energy of $2.29\mathrm{eV},$ which  is  in  close  resonance  with  the  incident $532\mathrm{-nm}$ laser $(\lambda_{\mathrm{L}}=2.33\mathrm{eV})$ , can be expected from the HOMO of dopamine to the  CBM  of  D-MIL- ${125-N\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ .  Notably,  vibronic  coupling among  the  aforementioned  computed  tomography  (CT) transitions (including $\mu_{\mathrm{GSCT}}$ and $\mu_{\mathrm{PICT}},$ ) through a Herzberg–Teller coupling  term[39]  may  contribute  to  the  significantly  improved SERS  enhancement  in $\mathrm{D}{\cdot}\mathrm{MIL}{\cdot}125{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(2\ \mathrm{h})$ compared  with pristine $\mathrm{MIL}{-}125{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{N}\mathrm{H}_{2},$ which  possessed  a  different  energy  band structure.
Moreover,  the  band-level  matching  between  the  MOFs  and analytes  may  be  insufficient  to  adequately  explain  the  enhanced SERS  activity,  whereas  improved  charge-transfer  ability  within MOF  substrate  enabled  by  photo-induced  defects  would  give  a further  contribution.  To  clarify  the  altered  charge  transfer behaviors  of ${\mathrm{D}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ with  respect  to  pristine  MIL-125- $\mathrm{NH}_{2},$ the intervalence charge transfer band revealed by the UVVis diffuse reflectance spectrum was considered. New absorption bands were clearly visible in the range of $1.55{-}2.50\mathrm{eV}$ for D-MIL${125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}},$ where  an  increase  in  signal  intensity  with  prolonged reaction time was indicated (Fig. 5(d)). The emerging absorptions in  D-MIL- ${125-N H_{2}}$ are  attributable  to  a  series  of  intervalence charge  transfer  bands,  which  suggest  mid-gap  polarons  and possibly  enhanced  charge  transport  properties  [22],  otherwise absent in pristine MIL- ${125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ . Accordingly, benefiting from the stabilized photo-induced vacancy defects, the vibronic coupling of multiple  CT  transitions $\operatorname{\mu}_{\operatorname{GSCT}}$ and $\mu_{\mathrm{PICT}})$ and  enhanced  charge transfer  within  MOFs  can  be  realized  simultaneously,  which  is responsible for the significantly enhanced SERS activity observed in the D-MIL- ${125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ substrate.
## 4    Discussion
In summary, the photo-induced vacancy defects can be stabilized by  the  photo-excitation  of  MOF  materials,  which  is  an  ideal material for evaluating their contributions to SERS performance. For instance, $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects can be maintained in D-MIL- ${125-N\mathrm{H}_{2}}$ for  more  than  2  weeks  under  ambient  atmosphere,  owing  to stabilization via steric hindrance and charge delocalization around vacancy  defects,  as  afforded  by  the  cluster-ligand  framework topologies of MOFs. With the formation of stable $\mathrm{PIV_{O}}$ defects, prominent  SERS  enhancement  surpassing  that  of  pristine  MIL${125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ was achieved in ${\mathrm{D}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125{\cdot}{\mathrm{NH}}_{2},$ , as evident from the detection of various analyte molecules, and the transcendence in SERS  activity  remained  distinguishable  even  after  120  days  of storage.  Furthermore,  the  vibronic  coupling  of  multiple  CT transitions  and  enhanced  charge  transfer  within  MOFs contributed to the significantly enhanced SERS activity observed on the D-MIL- ${125\mathrm{-NH}_{2}}$ substrate. More importantly, this strategy of photo-inducing stabilized oxygen vacancy defects in MOFs is widely  applicable  for  achieving  enhanced  SERS  performance  in MOF-based substrates.
